The one sentence you should remember

Space complexity measures how the memory required by an algorithm grows as the input size grows, especially the additional (auxiliary) memory used by the algorithm.

And the most useful question to ask yourself is:

"As n gets bigger, what am I storing that also gets bigger?"

If the answer is nothing → O(1).

If you're storing n things → O(n).
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The biggest mistakes to avoid
❌ "There is a loop, so space is O(n)."

Wrong.

A loop usually affects time, not necessarily space.

❌ "There are two nested loops, so space is O(n²)."

Wrong.

Only true if you're actually storing O(n²) information.

❌ "The input array is O(n), so the algorithm's space is O(n)."

Not necessarily.

The input may already be provided.

You generally report auxiliary space when discussing the algorithm's extra memory.

❌ "A recursive function doesn't create an array, so space is O(1)."

Wrong.

Recursive calls occupy the call stack.

❌ "O(n) means exactly n bytes."

Wrong.

Big-O describes growth, not exact memory consumption.
If you're storing n × n things → O(n²).

If recursion creates n active calls → O(n).
